I have received a few emails about my inquiry which lead me to believe a bit of explanation would be helpful.  The point I was trying to bring up is that legends are calculated according to the particular aesthetics used and, in the case of the size aesthetic, seem to be calculated incorrectly.  Notice in the below plots that the plots themselves are always correct, it is just that the legend in the last plot (using the size aesthetic) is generated using the attributes of the stat_function layer as opposed to the geom_point layer.

df <- data.frame(
  x = 1:10,
  y = 1:10,

  z = (1:10)/10
)

# using colour aesthetic, no problems
qplot(x, y, data = df, colour = z) +

  stat_function(fun = function(x) x, colour = 'red')

# using alpha aesthetic, no problems,
# although now the line appears, curiously
qplot(x, y, data = df, alpha = z) +

  stat_function(fun = function(x) x, colour = 'red')

# using size aesthetic throws the legend off completely
qplot(x, y, data = df, size = z) +

  stat_function(fun = function(x) x, colour = 'red')

Thanks for the comments
david.

