Hi David,

This is partly a bug and partly a graph misspecification.  The size
aesthetic is inherited by stat_function and even though the geom
correctly ignores it, the legend obviously doesn't.  Unfortunately
it's surprisingly hard to cover every edge case and get the legend
displaying correct, especially in cases (like this one) where the
graph specification doesn't really make sense.

Hadley

On Fri, Apr 16, 2010 at 12:19 PM, David Kahle <david...@gmail.com> wrote:
> I have received a few emails about my inquiry which lead me to believe a bit
> of explanation would be helpful.  The point I was trying to bring up is that
> legends are calculated according to the particular aesthetics used and, in
> the case of the size aesthetic, seem to be calculated incorrectly.  Notice
> in the below plots that the plots themselves are always correct, it is just
> that the legend in the last plot (using the size aesthetic) is generated
> using the attributes of the stat_function layer as opposed to the geom_point
> layer.
> df <- data.frame(
>   x = 1:10,
>   y = 1:10,
>   z = (1:10)/10
> )
> # using colour aesthetic, no problems
> qplot(x, y, data = df, colour = z) +
>   stat_function(fun = function(x) x, colour = 'red')
>
>
>
> # using alpha aesthetic, no problems,
> # although now the line appears, curiously
> qplot(x, y, data = df, alpha = z) +
>   stat_function(fun = function(x) x, colour = 'red')
>
>
>
> # using size aesthetic throws the legend off completely
> qplot(x, y, data = df, size = z) +
>   stat_function(fun = function(x) x, colour = 'red')
> Thanks for the comments
> david.
> On Apr 16, 2010, at 11:59 AM, fernando wrote:
>
> Hi David,
>
> Also complex to explain why, the code bellow worked for me. Think you should
> specify the size aesthetic for stat_function.
>
> library(ggplot2)
>
> df <- data.frame(
>   x = 1:10,
>   y = 1:10,
>   size = (1:10)/10
> )
>
> qplot(x, y, data = df, size = size) +
> stat_function(fun = function(x) x, colour = 'red', size = 0.5, legend =
> TRUE)
>
> # or
>
> ggplot(aes(x = x, y = y), data = df) +
>                 geom_line(aes(stat = stat_function(fun = function(x) x)),
> colour = "red", size = 0.5, legend = TRUE) +
>                 geom_point(aes(size = size))
>
> #sessionInfo()
> #R version 2.10.1 (2009-12-14)
> #i386-pc-mingw32
> #
> #locale:
> # [1] LC_COLLATE=Spanish_Spain.1252  LC_CTYPE=Spanish_Spain.1252
> # [3] LC_MONETARY=Spanish_Spain.1252 LC_NUMERIC=C
> # [5] LC_TIME=Spanish_Spain.1252
> #
> #attached base packages:
> # [1] grid      stats     graphics  grDevices utils     datasets  methods
> # [8] base
> #
> #other attached packages:
> # [1] ggplot2_0.8.7 digest_0.4.2  reshape_0.8.3 plyr_0.1.9    proto_0.3-8
>
>
> De: ggp...@googlegroups.com [mailto:ggp...@googlegroups.com] En nombre
> de David Kahle
> Enviado el: viernes, 16 de abril de 2010 18:13
> Para: ggplot2
> Asunto: Legend bug?
>
> See below (it's easier than describing :) -- the items in the legend are
> being attributed to the stat_function layer instead of the size aesthetic.
> Or, am I missing something?
>
> df <- data.frame(
>   x = 1:10,
>   y = 1:10,
>   size = (1:10)/10
> )
> qplot(x, y, data = df, size = size) +
